<grounded_cve_verification_guide>
<title>Grounded CVE Verification</title>

<critical>A CVE claim without evidence is a hallucination risk, not a finding. Verify every CVE against the fingerprinted version of the target using the pinned knowledge base. No invented CVE IDs, versions, or CVSS scores - ever.</critical>

<scope>
- Version fingerprinting: headers, banners, error pages, JS bundles, and package metadata that reveal component and version
- CVE matching: resolving a fingerprint to CVE records via cve_lookup / cve_match against the pinned KB
- Applicability: whether the vulnerable version range includes the fingerprinted version and the component is reachable
</scope>

<methodology>
1. Fingerprint first: collect version evidence from primary sources only (HTTP headers, body content, tool output). Record the exact evidence_id and the observed string.
2. Match second: call cve_match (or cve_lookup) with the fingerprinted component and version. Never state a CVE ID from memory or from web search alone.
3. Verify applicability: confirm the fingerprinted version falls in the CVE's affected range, and check whether the component is actually present/reachable in the target.
4. Record the full grounding tuple per CVE claim:
   - evidence_id: the primary evidence record supporting the fingerprint
   - fingerprint: component name + observed version (exact string from evidence)
   - references: CVE record references returned by the KB lookup (advisory URLs, IDs)
5. If the KB has no matching record, report "no matching CVE in pinned KB" - do not substitute a CVE from memory or training data.
6. Version disclosure alone is an informational observation, never a CVE finding. A CVE finding requires steps 1-4.
</methodology>

<grounding_rules>
- Every CVE, version, and CVSS claim must resolve in the pinned KB via cve_lookup/cve_match.
- Evidence chain per claim: evidence_id + fingerprint + references. Missing any element means the claim is unverified.
- Web search and model memory cannot confirm a CVE; they can only suggest what to look up.
- When evidence conflicts (for example a header vs a bundle version), report both evidence_ids and mark the claim disputed.
</grounding_rules>

<checklist>
- [ ] Version obtained from primary evidence with evidence_id recorded
- [ ] CVE resolved via cve_match/cve_lookup, not memory
- [ ] Affected version range includes the fingerprinted version
- [ ] Component reachability assessed
- [ ] Grounding tuple complete: evidence_id + fingerprint + references
</checklist>
</grounded_cve_verification_guide>
